    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Cell biology functions and pathways of the major genes involved in melanoma susceptibility",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<em>CDKN2A</em> produces two proteins, p16 (p16INK4a) and p14ARF, essential for controlling the cell cycle and inducing apoptosis in damaged cells, respectively. Mutations in <em>CDKN2A</em> or <em>CDK4</em> disrupt this balance, potentially leading to uncontrolled cell proliferation and tumour development. <em>BAP1</em> is primarily involved in epigenetic and gene transcription regulation. When mutated, it can disrupt proliferation controls, potentially causing abnormal cell proliferation and tumour growth. The shelterin complex, which includes POT1, protects chromosome ends and regulates telomerase access to telomeres. Mutations in the shelterin genes can result in telomere dysregulation, potentially triggering cell senescence, apoptosis, or tumour development.",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Adapted, with kind permission from AME Publishing Company, from: Potrony M, Badenas C, Aguilera P, et al. Update in genetic susceptibility in melanoma. Ann Transl Med. 2015 Sep;3(15):210. PMID:26488006.",
